Feature: Structural mechanics kernel
  Closed-form results and independent third-party oracles bound the kernel; progress, cancellation and determinism are part of the contract.

  Further specification assertions, asserted by the Rust unit tests of the crate and not by the platform adapters:
  - Euler column <support> buckles at the closed-form load factor: Given a steel column of length 5 m with E I = 16.8e6 N m2 divided into 16 elements and support "<support>" When the linear buckling eigenproblem is solved for a unit compressive load Then the critical load factor equals <factor> times pi squared E I over L squared within 1e-3 Examples (4 rows: pinned-pinned-1, fixed-free-0-25, fixed-pinned-2-04576, fixed-fixed-4)
  - Simply supported beam mode <mode> has the closed-form frequency: Given a simply supported steel beam of length 6 m with E I = 16.8e6 N m2 and 78.5 kg per metre in 16 elements When the modal analysis with consistent mass is solved Then the frequency of mode <mode> equals <factor> times pi over 2 L squared times sqrt(E I over m) within 1e-3 Examples (3 rows: 1-1, 2-4, 3-9)
  - Uniform moment lateral-torsional buckling equals the closed form: Given a fork-supported I-shaped member with the IPE-like constants of the oracle script When the lateral-torsional eigenproblem is solved for a uniform moment Then the critical moment equals the Timoshenko closed form within 1e-4
  - Static results match PyNiteFEA, anastruct and scipy: Given the reference beam, portal, L-frame, column and LTB cases of the oracle script When the kernel and the oracle solve them Then every compared quantity agrees within the stated tolerance
  - Cancellation and determinism: Given a stability analysis with a cancel flag already raised Then the analysis ends with the cancelled error And two runs without cancellation return identical results
  - Member modes reproduce the Euler loads and beam deflections: Given a prismatic member in its own frame with pinned, cantilever and fixed end conditions When the buckling load and the deflection line are computed Then Ncr equals pi squared EI over the effective length squared within 0.2 percent for all three end conditions And the deflection under uniform load, point load, end couples and a tip load equals the textbook formulas within 0.1 percent
  - Cross-section constants match sectionproperties: Given a rectangle, sharp I, channel, angle, RHS, CHS and a polygon with a hexagonal hole as vertex lists, and the IPE300, PFC200, L100x10, tee, plate-girder, rounded RHS and elliptical hollow section profile outlines When the finite-element section solver meshes and solves them Then area, centroid, second moments and elastic and plastic moduli agree within 1e-3 percent, and torsion constant, warping constant, shear centre and both shear areas agree with the FE library sectionproperties within 0.5 percent
  - Cross-section constants have closed forms: Given a rectangle, a circular hollow section and a doubly symmetric thin-walled I When the section is analysed Then the torsion constant equals the Prandtl series within 0.2 percent, the shear area equals five sixths of the area for a Poisson ratio of zero, the circular section has no warping and the thin-walled I has J equal to the sum of l t cubed over three and I_w equal to t_f b cubed h squared over 24
  - Thin-walled solver is consistent with the finite-element solver: Given I, channel, angle and tee centreline models and their sharp-cornered polygons When both solvers run Then torsion constant is within 4 percent, warping constant within 3 percent, shear centre within 1 mm and shear areas within 10 percent, and closed or disconnected walls are refused
  - Section analysis is interactive and deterministic: Given a polygon with a hole When it is analysed with a progress sink and with a raised cancel flag Then the stages mesh, assemble, factorise, solve and integrate are reported with fractions in 0..1, the cancelled error ends the analysis, and two runs return identical constants
  - Fastening kernel reproduces the EN 1992-4 closed forms: Given the language-agnostic cases of the fastening kernel (cone breakout with edge, pair and eccentricity, pull-out, steel factors, edge breakout, pry-out, interaction) When the kernel and the oracle script evaluate them with annex rows passed in as parameters Then every quantity equals the stated closed-form value and the shapely footprint union within 1e-9 relative
  - Fibre section kernel integrates strain planes and finds the yield and ultimate points: Given a rectangular reinforced concrete section of 300 mm by 500 mm with a parabola-rectangle concrete law without tension, elastic perfectly plastic bars at 450 mm and 50 mm depth, and the axial forces 0 and 300 kN When the moment-curvature curve and the first-limit yield and ultimate points are computed Then a linear law integrates to E A and E I, the linear cracked section yields at the closed-form curvature and moment within 0.3 percent, the yield and ultimate strain planes sit on their strain limits, the curve rises to the ultimate moment, and the moments at four curvatures, the ultimate moment and the ultimate curvature agree with the finite-element fibre library concreteproperties within 0.5 percent
  - Moment line M(x) has closed forms and matches numpy: Given simply supported, cantilever, propped and n-span continuous beams under uniform and point loads When the three-moment moment line is solved Then it equals wL squared over 8, wL squared over 2, minus wL squared over 8 over the middle support, 9 wL squared over 128 in the span and 5 wL over 8 at a propped root, vertical equilibrium holds, and 240 random beams agree with the numpy slope-deflection oracle at 13 abscissae, every support moment and every reaction within 1e-9 relative
